<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android network security config, applied by expo-network-security-config (see app.json).

  Lyftr is self-hosted: the backend is usually a LAN box on plain HTTP, or HTTPS with a
  certificate from the owner's own CA installed on their device. Android's release defaults
  block both — cleartext since API 28, user-installed CAs since API 24 — so the documented
  "point the app at http://<lan-ip>:8080" setup could not work at all (issue #79).

  This opts into exactly those two things and nothing else. HTTPS is still fully validated
  (chain, expiry, hostname/SAN); the only change is that roots the device owner deliberately
  installed also count. It is NOT "ignore certificate errors".

  base-config applies app-wide rather than being scoped to a <domain-config>, which is NOT
  what Android recommends. We can't follow that guidance: the server URL is entered by the
  user at runtime and this file is resolved at build time, so there is no hostname to
  enumerate. Scoping trust to the configured host needs a runtime X509TrustManager /
  NSURLSession delegate — tracked in #81. Until then the app compensates with visibility:
  isInsecureServerUrl in @lyftr/shared drives an amber "Not encrypted" marker on every
  server-URL surface, and the docs lead with HTTPS.

  Changes here only take effect in a NEW build — EAS Update cannot deliver them.
-->
<network-security-config>
  <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
    <trust-anchors>
      <certificates src="system" />
      <certificates src="user" />
    </trust-anchors>
  </base-config>
</network-security-config>
